    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d274">

                <pb facs="0378" n="372" sameAs="frus:frus1901China/pg372"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Rockhill to Mr. Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1901-05-25"><hi rendition="#italic">May 25,
                        1901</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>(Mr. Rockhill reports that all the powers will without a doubt soon agree to
                    limit the idemnity to 450,000,000 taels, and that Germany, Great Britain, and
                    Italy have already done so officially. Expresses the <pb facs="0379" n="373"
                        xml:id="pg373"/> opinion that a transfer of the question to The Hague would
                    have no other use than to cause delay, and asks whether he should make a
                    proposition to so transfer it or agree to the amount as limited. Inquires
                    whether the United States would agree to a proposition of the Japanese to the
                    British Government tending to the abolition of all likin dues on imports to be
                    compensated by an advance to 10 per cent of Chinese tariff on imports. Suggests
                    that other compensations might be obtained in addition.)</p>
            </div>
        </body>
